Issue #780: [M3][#773] Preserve versioned human+AI artifact state, locks, guidance, and branch control

Parent: #773
Depends on: #774 CreativeBrief; #775 creative DAG; #777 mixed human/agent control
Related: #779 cross-artifact evaluation; canonical artifact/provenance owner

## Purpose

Make direct editing, AI collaboration, autonomous generation, interruption and resumption operate on one durable artifact/change model.

The user must never have to choose between "manual artifact" and "agent artifact" representations, and autonomous work must never erase human edits or accepted versions.

## Version semantics

Every material change to a creative artifact or shared creative decision must be attributable to a durable version/change record that can identify:
- parent/base version;
- producing human or canonical Run/NodeRun/Attempt/Invocation where applicable;
- CreativeBrief version and relevant shared-decision inputs;
- timestamp and provenance;
- accepted/rejected/draft state where applicable;
- explicit user lock/freeze state;
- supersession/fork relationships rather than destructive overwrite.

The implementation should reuse the canonical Artifact/provenance contract rather than inventing a competing universal artifact store.

## Human edits

Manual edits are first-class. They must:
- preserve prior AI-generated versions;
- become visible to the Workspace Agent before subsequent eligible work;
- be usable as new DAG inputs/constraints;
- retain author/provenance without pretending they were model output;
- optionally be promoted to an accepted/locked decision or artifact version.

## Locks / freezes

A user may lock:
- a whole artifact version;
- a shared decision/message/claim;
- a region/component/section where the artifact model supports stable identity;
- an artifact branch pending review.

Autonomous refinement may flag a conflict with a lock but may not silently rewrite it. Changing/removing a lock is an explicit user action or separately authorized policy action.

## Guidance / redirect

User guidance during active work becomes durable project input. It can narrow/redirect future work and cause dependency-aware invalidation through #775, without deleting previous results.

## Branch control

At one moment, different branches may be:
- directly user-controlled;
- collaborative;
- autonomous;
- paused;
- locked/frozen;
- waiting for approval;
- cancelling/stopping;
- complete.

Control state is product/domain state projected onto canonical execution; it is not a second scheduler/lifecycle.

## Acceptance

- [ ] AI generates an artifact, user manually edits it, agent resumes refinement from the edited version, and all three versions remain inspectable with correct provenance.
- [ ] Locking an accepted artifact prevents autonomous replacement while allowing unrelated branches to continue.
- [ ] Locking a shared decision constrains all future descendants that reference it.
- [ ] Redirect guidance during an active project is durable across refresh/reconnect and becomes visible to newly eligible agent work.
- [ ] User can fork an artifact/creative direction without erasing the original branch.
- [ ] Paused/controlled/locked states survive browser refresh and process restart where their underlying canonical state is durable.
- [ ] Conflicts between a requested refinement and a lock are surfaced explicitly rather than silently dropping the lock or pretending the refinement succeeded.
- [ ] Manual edits and AI edits use the same artifact representation and export path.
- [ ] E2E proves a mixed-control project with simultaneous direct, autonomous and locked branches.

## Stop condition

Do not create a second universal Artifact/version store or a Design-Studio-private execution lifecycle. Product-specific editor state may exist only as a projection/extension of canonical artifact and execution provenance.

