Issue #38: Support conditional and path-aware nodes

## Problem

Every declared node is generated for every supported pull request or push. Repositories cannot express that an expensive gate is relevant only when selected paths change.

Examples include:

- browser and frontend performance checks;
- bundle-size checks;
- Docker image builds;
- product E2E suites;
- language-specific checks in polyglot repositories.

Always running these nodes wastes runner time and makes unrelated changes depend on expensive or environment-sensitive infrastructure. Hiding the condition inside a shell command also loses graph-level semantics: the dashboard cannot distinguish "not applicable" from a successful execution, and dependencies still consume a runner job.

## Proposed capability

Support declarative node applicability using validated conditions and path filters.

A possible shape:

```yaml
nodes:
  frontend-performance:
    title: Frontend performance regression
    needs: [build]
    when:
      paths:
        - web/**
        - tools/frontend-perf/**
        - deploy/Dockerfile.front
      paths-ignore:
        - web/**/*.md
    run: make perf-front-diff
```

And, where path matching is insufficient:

```yaml
nodes:
  integration:
    if: graph.event == 'pull_request' && nodes.scope.outputs.integration == 'true'
    run: make integration
```

The exact syntax is open for design.

## Required semantics

- determine applicability before allocating an expensive runner where the provider supports it;
- expose a stable terminal `not-applicable` or `skipped` state;
- treat an inapplicable blocking node as satisfied, not failed;
- propagate dependencies deterministically;
- render the reason in dashboards and manifests;
- preserve behavior for partial reruns;
- define pull-request, push, merge-queue, and manual-dispatch semantics;
- support added, modified, renamed, and deleted files;
- evaluate paths against the merge base, not an arbitrary shallow checkout;
- remain fork-safe and require no secrets.

## Validation

- reject contradictory or empty path declarations;
- validate references used by conditional expressions;
- detect dependency configurations that can never execute;
- include applicability policy in the graph digest and manifest.

## Acceptance criteria

- a frontend performance node can be skipped for a documentation-only PR without starting its runner;
- downstream nodes receive deterministic results when a dependency is not applicable;
- dashboards distinguish pass from not applicable;
- partial reruns cannot force an invalid node unless explicitly supported;
- schema, compiler, manifest, docs, and provider tests cover path and expression conditions.

## Context

Found while migrating alchemmist/monori#424. Browser performance and bundle-size gates had to run for a CI-only migration because Quality Graph 0.1.2 cannot represent their existing change scope.

