Issue #2: Research: Parallel coding agents in real product work

## Core question

When does splitting real product work across multiple coding agents produce meaningful throughput gains, and what decomposition, isolation, coordination, verification, and merge practices make it work without creating more overhead or risk than it saves?

## Why investigate this now

The current human → thinking assistant → coding agent workflow is working well enough that eliminating the handoff is not a priority. A potentially higher-leverage next step is understanding when multiple implementation agents can safely work at once.

This research should focus on what practitioners are actually doing repeatedly on real codebases, including failure modes—not just multi-agent demos or theoretical orchestration architectures.

## Initial findings — 2026-08-30

### 1. Parallel agents are already normal among power users, not merely experimental demos

OpenAI's Codex app explicitly supports multiple agents in parallel with isolated worktrees. OpenAI reports that by June 2026, its heaviest internal Codex users were generating more than 60 hours of agent turns per day, distributed across multiple parallel agents.

Anthropic's current Claude Code power-user guidance calls 3–5 parallel sessions, each in its own git worktree, its biggest productivity unlock. Claude Code also supports isolated subagents and large `/batch` fan-out.

### 2. The common practical unit is an isolated task/workspace, not several agents editing one checkout

The recurring pattern is:

- identify genuinely independent work;
- give each task/agent its own branch/worktree/workspace;
- give each a clear written objective/spec;
- let each validate its own result;
- reconcile/merge through a controlled boundary.

Practitioner reports consistently say that simply opening multiple sessions is easy. The difficulty begins when their changes overlap or when several completed branches need to be landed together.

### 3. Independence is the fundamental constraint

Anthropic's 16-agent C-compiler experiment demonstrated this unusually clearly. Parallelism worked well when there were many distinct failing tests or independent compilation targets. When all 16 agents hit the same Linux-kernel blocker, they duplicated effort and overwrote one another; adding agents provided no benefit until the problem was redesigned into independently diagnosable slices.

This suggests a key hypothesis for normal product development:

> Agent count does not create parallelism. The dependency structure of the work creates parallelism.

### 4. Worktrees are emerging as the default isolation mechanism—but do not solve coordination

Both OpenAI and Anthropic now have native worktree-oriented multi-agent workflows. Practitioners commonly use one worktree/branch per agent.

The failure reports are also consistent: worktrees prevent file clobbering, but they do not prevent incompatible architectural decisions, stale assumptions, conflicting refactors, merge-order problems, or a combined result that fails even though each branch passed independently.

A particularly common pattern is: parallel coding succeeds, then landing the branches becomes the bottleneck.

### 5. Humans appear to hit an attention ceiling around 3–5 directly supervised agents

OpenAI describes the experience that led to Symphony: engineers could comfortably supervise roughly 3–5 Codex sessions before context switching became painful. Their solution was not a better terminal layout; it was to stop treating sessions as the control plane.

Symphony instead maps open tasks to isolated agent workspaces, uses the task tracker as the state machine/control plane, respects task dependencies, and lets humans review results. OpenAI reports up to a 500% increase in landed PRs on some teams after adopting it.

This is materially different from simply running five terminal tabs.

### 6. Issue/task graphs are becoming an orchestration primitive

OpenAI's Symphony is especially relevant to our current direction. Its model is effectively:

- task tracker holds durable work and dependency state;
- each eligible open task gets an isolated workspace and agent;
- blocked tasks wait;
- unblocked tasks can execute concurrently;
- agents can create follow-up tasks;
- CI/rebase/retry/merge handling can be automated;
- humans remain the review/acceptance boundary where appropriate.

That strongly resembles the product-dependency-graph principle already discovered in PedalFish, but takes the next step of using that graph to drive execution automatically.

### 7. Specialized parallel agents are also useful even when implementation itself should stay serial

A second recurring pattern is using parallel agents for independent perspectives rather than independent code changes: plan review, security review, tests, documentation, performance analysis, architecture critique, and code-quality review. Anthropic uses multi-agent review internally and describes review becoming a bottleneck as code output per engineer increased.

This may be a lower-risk way to gain value from multiple agents before attempting heavily parallel implementation.

## Failure modes to investigate further

- two agents changing the same architectural seam;
- one agent refactoring an interface while another builds against the old one;
- duplicated work because agents cannot see each other's intent/progress;
- individually green branches failing when combined;
- merge/rebase order becoming the new bottleneck;
- human attention becoming saturated by agent supervision;
- agents making incompatible local architectural decisions;
- shared external resources (databases, Preview environments, ports, credentials) defeating filesystem isolation;
- extra agent usage exceeding the value of the elapsed-time savings.

