Issue #220: bug(orch): two conflicting topoSort implementations — one throws on cycle, one records and continues

## Bug

`engine/orch/runner/dag.ts` — two conflicting topological sort implementations with different cycle behavior:

**`topologicalSort`** (line 10) — records cycle, continues:
```ts
if (temp.has(id)) {
  const cycleStart = path.indexOf(id);
  cycles.push(path.slice(cycleStart));
  return;  // ← continues visiting other nodes
}
```

**`topoSort`** (line 80) — throws immediately:
```ts
if (temp.has(id)) {
  cycle.push(...path.slice(start));
  return false;  // ← propagates up
}
// ...
for (const id of nodeIds) {
  if (!visit(id, [])) {
    throw new CycleError(cycle);  // ← throws on first cycle
  }
}
```

Both exist in the same file. `DefaultOrchestratorRunner.run()` uses `topologicalSort` (runner.ts:36) — which silently continues past cycles. A cyclic graph produces an `order` with a cycle embedded in it, and `hasCycles: true` is set, but execution continues.

If `topoSort` were used instead, the runner would throw `CycleError` immediately.

## Exploitation

```ts
const dag = buildDag({ raw: "a,b;a", tokens: [], constraints: [] });
// cyclic edges: s0→s1, s1→s0
const runner = new DefaultOrchestratorRunner();
const result = await runner.run(dag, {});
// topologicalSort continues past cycle → executes with wrong order
// hasCycles = true but no error thrown
```

## Fix

Remove `topoSort` (dead code), or merge both into one implementation with a configurable policy.


