## Why

neurOS has accumulated unusually strong runtime, replay, provenance, Scientific Authority, Model Artifact, interoperability and synthetic falsification contracts. The central risk is now **breadth without an externally indispensable scientific wedge**.

Established projects already own major parts of the ecosystem:

- MNE-Python: neurophysiology preprocessing, analysis, visualization and statistics;
- Braindecode: end-to-end deep neural decoding and published model implementations;
- MOABB: reproducible EEG BCI dataset/pipeline benchmarking;
- BIDS/NWB: community data organization and neurophysiology standards;
- BrainFlow/LSL/BCI2000: device acquisition, transport and real-time experimental execution;
- SpikeInterface: interoperable extracellular-electrophysiology processing/benchmarking;
- Meta NeuroAI: scalable data loading/fetching/training/benchmarking;
- Brain-Score: biological model-alignment benchmarks;
- NeuroBench: algorithm/system benchmarking including system-aware efficiency.

neurOS should not compete by reimplementing those commodity surfaces.

## Product/scientific thesis

> **neurOS should become the open qualification layer for neural AI systems:** the place where an existing dataset, model, runtime, device, adaptation procedure and scientific claim are bound into a reproducible, falsifiable, evidence-graded system artifact.

ORION is the neural-intelligence plane used to test whether representations/tokenizers/adaptation actually improve transfer and reduce calibration under this qualification authority.

The core value is not another model zoo or preprocessing stack. It is making questions such as these executable and comparable:

- Was the deployment subject/session/device unseen under the exact declared training/pretraining lineage?
- How many labeled/unlabeled target observations were consumed before final assessment?
- Did preprocessing, calibration or adaptation leak final-assessment information?
- Does an accuracy improvement survive session, subject, montage, artifact and device shifts?
- Is a probability actually calibrated, or merely a softmax score?
- Is the selected model artifact exactly the one qualified and deployed?
- Can the complete experiment be replayed and independently reproduced?
- Which part of a claim is supported by software, real-data, hardware, closed-loop or clinical evidence?
- What breaks under realistic synthetic counterexamples before expensive human/device experiments?

