Issue #3520: feat: High-Level Architecture Design: Workspace Dependency Graph & Run Triggers in Terrakube

### Feature description 💡

## 1. Executive Summary

In complex cloud infrastructure architectures, infrastructure components are modularized across distinct workspaces (e.g., `vpc-networking` &rarr; `database-clusters` &rarr; `application-workloads`). A successful run in an upstream workspace must automatically trigger downstream dependent workspaces to propagate changes (such as state outputs, VPC IDs, or database connection strings) without manual intervention.

This architecture introduces **Upstream Run Triggers** to Terrakube. By configuring run triggers, workspaces declare dependencies on upstream source workspaces. When a job modifying an upstream workspace finishes with `completed` status, the Terrakube Run Trigger engine evaluates the dependency graph, enforces cycle protection, and reactively enqueues downstream jobs. These downstream jobs are then dispatched through Terrakube's existing distributed locking and two-tier FIFO queue.

```mermaid
graph TD
    subgraph Organization
        WS_NET["Workspace: Networking (VPC)"]
        WS_DB["Workspace: Database (RDS)"]
        WS_APP1["Workspace: API Service"]
        WS_APP2["Workspace: Web Frontend"]

        WS_NET -->|"Run Trigger (on apply completed)"| WS_DB
        WS_NET -->|"Run Trigger (on apply completed)"| WS_APP1
        WS_DB -->|"Run Trigger (on apply completed)"| WS_APP1
        WS_APP1 -->|"Run Trigger (on apply completed)"| WS_APP2
    end

    classDef ws fill:#1e293b,stroke:#3b82f6,stroke-width:2px,color:#fff;
    class WS_NET,WS_DB,WS_APP1,WS_APP2 ws;
```

---

## 2. Key Design Decisions

Following our architectural discovery and design tree resolution, the system adheres to the following principles:

| Decision Area | Selected Design Choice | Rationale |
|---|---|---|
| **Model Architecture** | **Upstream Run Triggers** (`WorkspaceRunTrigger`) | Downstream workspaces declare subscriptions to upstream workspaces, creating a decentralized, composable DAG without requiring rigid monolithic pipeline definitions. |
| **Trigger Qualification** | **State-Changing Runs Only** (`apply` / `destroy` `completed`) | Pure speculative plans do not modify state and must not trigger downstream runs. Only runs that change infrastructure state qualify. |
| **Multi-Upstream Synchronization** | **Independent Reactive Triggering** | Each upstream completion immediately triggers the downstream workspace. Terrakube's existing workspace-level queue safely serializes multiple executions on the same workspace. |
| **Cycle & Loop Safeguards** | **Static DFS Cycle Detection + Runtime Hop Limit** | Static graph validation prevents cycles at configuration time (HTTP 400); runtime causation tracking (`cascadeDepth <= 10`) guarantees termination against unexpected cascades. |
| **Boundary Scope** | **Intra-Organization** | Dependencies can span any workspaces within the same Organization (including across different Projects), maintaining tenant isolation while supporting cross-project architecture. |
| **Execution Template** | **Downstream Default Template (with optional trigger override)** | Executes the downstream workspace's `defaultTemplate` by default, but allows overriding the template in the `WorkspaceRunTrigger` configuration. |
| **Security & Identity** | **`serviceAccount` with Setup Permissions** | Creating/updating a trigger requires `team manage workspace` on destination and `team view workspace` on source; automated executions run under system `serviceAccount` with `JobVia.RUN_TRIGGER`. |
| **Execution Lineage** | **Causation Tracking & DAG Querying** | Downstream jobs store `triggeredByJobId` and `cascadeDepth`, enabling end-to-end traceability and DAG visualization in UI and API. |

---

